<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"><!--
    Shared properties come from Directory.Build.props; versions from
    Directory.Packages.props. Project references are the ring rule made executable -
    see the allowed-edge table in Yello.Tests.Architecture/AllowedReferenceEdges.cs.
  -->

  <!--
    Ring 3, and the only ring below Host where EF Core and ASP.NET Core Identity may be
    referenced at all. AllowedReferenceEdges.ForbiddenPackagePrefixes bans both prefixes from
    Domain, Application, Contracts and Merge; this project and Yello.Host are the exceptions,
    because this is where the adapters live.

    No Version attributes: central package management supplies them, and
    PackageVersionPinTests.No_project_declares_a_package_version_of_its_own gates that.
    All four EF Core and Identity pins were already in Directory.Packages.props, labelled
    "first referenced by story 1.3" - this is that story, and the pins are unchanged.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="AR-1: EF Core 10 and Identity 10 - first referenced here, story 1.3">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" />
    <!--
      Design is a development-time dependency: it is what `dotnet ef migrations add` loads to
      build the model and scaffold a migration. PrivateAssets="all" keeps it out of the
      published output and out of every transitive consumer, which is the shape the EF Core
      templates use and the reason it does not reach Yello.Host at runtime.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <!--
      Referenced for exactly one type: PasswordHasher<T>, which lives in the
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity namespace and reaches here through this package.

      NOT for IdentityDbContext, and that is the point worth stating. IdentityDbContext would
      create AspNetUsers, AspNetRoles, AspNetUserRoles and four more tables - and there is no
      Users table in this architecture (the entity is Account), while every role table is
      precisely what AD-1 and Gate C exist to keep out. The package is the shipping vehicle for
      the hasher; none of the rest of it is wired, and Gate C's IL scan is what keeps that true
      rather than this comment.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    IStringLocalizer, for the one server-side resource this story reads: the Personal Space name
    template. See the pin's comment in Directory.Packages.props for why it is a new central pin
    rather than an inherited framework reference.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Localisation">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Localization" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The adapters here are internal on purpose: a public AccountRegistrationStore is one a later
    story can reach past its port, which is the whole thing the ports exist to prevent. The slice
    tests still have to construct them, because a test that exercised only the public surface
    would be testing the DI container rather than the adapter.

    InternalsVisibleTo to exactly one project, not a blanket. Yello.Tests.Slices is where slice
    and integration tests live; nothing else needs this, and widening it would quietly turn
    "internal" into "public with extra steps".
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Yello.Tests.Slices" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Yello.Application\Yello.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Yello.Domain\Yello.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
